 "drivingQuestion": "Why did the Sages institute that a hired worker swears and collects?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Shmuel's terminology is emended; the specific reason for the hired worker is not yet given within this unit.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.45a.12",
   "daf": "45a",
   "sentenceId": 11,
   "he": "וְאֵלּוּ נִשְׁבָּעִין וְנוֹטְלִין כּוּ׳. מַאי שְׁנָא שָׂכִיר דְּתַקִּינוּ לֵיה רַבָּנַן דְּמִשְׁתְּבַע וְשָׁקֵיל? אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה אָמַר שְׁמוּאֵל: הֲלָכוֹת גְּדוֹלוֹת שָׁנוּ כָּאן. הֲלָכוֹת? הָנֵי הִלְכְתָא נִינְהוּ?! אֶלָּא אֵימָא: תַּקָּנוֹת גְּדוֹלוֹת שָׁנוּ כָּאן.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And these» litigants «take an oath and receive» possession of the disputed funds or property, and it lists a hired worker in that category. The Gemara asks: «What is different» about «a hired worker that the Sages instituted for him that he take an oath and receive» his wages? «Rav Yehuda said» that «Shmuel said: Great halakhot were taught here.» The Gemara asks: «Halakhot? Are these» oaths actually «halakhot» transmitted to Moses from Sinai, as is usually indicated by the use of the term halakhot? They are instituted by rabbinic law. «Rather, say: Great ordinances were taught here.»"
  }
 ],
